1. Introduction

1.1. The Navier–Stokes equations

We consider the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations in three dimensions:

\begin{cases}
\partial_t u +(u\cdot\nabla)u=\nu\Delta u-\nabla p,\\[2pt]
\nabla\cdot u=0,\\[2pt]
u(0,x)=u_0(x),
\end{cases}
\tag{1.1}

with normalization  \nu=1 . The global regularity problem remains open.

1.2. Changes from Version 5.0

Version 5.1 addresses the three principal technical vulnerabilities identified in the critical review of Version 5.0:

1. Off-diagonal heat-kernel decay for Lemma 6.1: A new lemma (Lemma 6.2) establishes explicit geometric decay of far-field contributions to the dyadic Carleson summation, with rate  2^{-k}  on dyadic shells  C_k(Q) . This closes the nonlocality gap in the proof of Lemma 6.1.
2. Paraproduct decomposition of Conjecture 5.1: The dyadic bilinear conjecture is now decomposed via Bony's algorithm into low-high and high-high components. The low-high terms are controlled directly by the  \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}  norm without scale losses; the resonant high-high term is controlled by sub-dyadic dissipation with geometric convergence  \sum_{k\ge j} 2^{-5k/4} , yielding  \delta = 3/4  in Conjecture 5.1.
3. Unconditional axisymmetric benchmark: Target 10.1 is now proved unconditionally in the no-swirl case via the global maximum principle for Γ. The local Carleson average satisfies  \left(\frac{1}{|Q|}\int_{Q^*} |\Gamma|^2\right)^{1/2} \le \ell(Q)\|\Gamma_0\|_{L^\infty} , providing the first concrete verification of the dyadic Carleson framework.

