2. Notation and Function Spaces

2.1. Littlewood–Paley decomposition

 \Delta_j  denotes the standard homogeneous dyadic frequency projection with Fourier support in  \{2^{j-1}\le|\xi|\le 2^{j+1}\} .

2.2. Homogeneous Besov space  \dot{B}^{-1}_{\infty,\infty} 

\|f\|_{\dot{B}^{-1}_{\infty,\infty}} = \sup_{j\in\mathbb Z} 2^{-j}\|\Delta_j f\|_{L^\infty}.
\tag{2.1}

2.3. Koch–Tataru space  \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}  and dyadic variant

The Koch–Tataru norm is

\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}}
=
\sup_{x\in\mathbb R^3,\,R>0}
\left(
\frac{1}{|B(x,R)|}
\int_0^{R^2}
\int_{B(x,R)}
|e^{t\Delta}f(y)|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{2.2}

The dyadic Carleson norm is

\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}
=
\sup_{Q\in\mathcal D}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_0^{\ell(Q)^2}
\int_Q
|e^{t\Delta}f(y)|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2},
\tag{2.3}

where  \mathcal D  denotes the family of all dyadic cubes in  \mathbb R^3  and  \ell(Q)  is the side length of  Q . The continuous and dyadic Carleson conditions are equivalent:

C^{-1}\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}}
\le
\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}
\le
C\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}}.
\tag{2.4}

2.4. Local dissipation functionals

For a parabolic cylinder

Q^* = Q \times (t-\ell(Q)^2,t),
\qquad Q\in\mathcal D,

we define

\mathcal E_Q(t)
=
\left(
\int_{Q^*} |\nabla u(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2},
\qquad
\varepsilon_Q(t)
=
\mathcal E_Q(t)^2.
\tag{2.5}

For frequency  j  and spatial cube  Q  of side length  2^{-j} :

D_{j,Q}(t)
=
\int_{Q^*}
2^{2j}|\Delta_j u(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds.
\tag{2.6}

Bernstein's inequality gives  D_{j,Q}(t) \le C\mathcal E_Q(t)^2 = C\varepsilon_Q(t) .

