3. Dyadic Bilinear Conjecture and Non-Concentration Hypothesis

3.1. Dyadic frequency-localized dissipation-moderated bilinear conjecture

Conjecture 3.1 (Dyadic bilinear estimate).
There exist absolute constants  C>0 ,  \delta>0 ,  \theta>0 , and  c>0  such that for every divergence-free vector field

u\in L^\infty_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,T);\dot{B}^{-1}_{\infty,\infty})\cap L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,T);\dot H^1),

for every  j\ge j_0 , every  t\in(0,T) , and with  \tau_j=c2^{-2j} , one has

2^{j(\frac12+\delta)}
\left\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\right\|_{L^2([t-\tau_j,t];L^2)}
\le
C
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
N(t)
\,
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t),
\tag{3.1}

where  N(t)=\sup_{s\le t}\|u(s)\|_{\dot{B}^{-1}_{\infty,\infty}} .

3.2. Scale-localized dissipation non-concentration hypothesis

Hypothesis 3.2 (Dyadic non-concentration).
Let  \delta,\theta  be as in Conjecture 3.1 and let  C_1  be the constant appearing in Theorem A below. If  T^*<\infty  is a finite maximal time of existence, then there exist  j_1\ge1  and  \eta>0  such that

\sup_{t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*)}
\sup_{j\ge j_1}
2^{-j\delta}
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t)
\le
\frac{1}{4C_1}.
\tag{3.2}

