4. Conditional Besov Boundedness Theorem

Theorem A (Conditional Besov boundedness).
Assume Conjecture 3.1 and Hypothesis 3.2 hold. Let  u_0\in L^2_\sigma\cap \mathrm{BMO}^{-1} , and let  u  be the unique local mild solution. If  T^*<\infty  is a finite maximal time of existence, then there exist  \eta>0  and  C_0>0  such that

\sup_{t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*)} N(t)
\le
C_0.
\tag{4.1}

Moreover, the same conclusion holds on any compact subinterval of existence.

4.1. Proof of Theorem A

Fix  j_1,\eta  as in Hypothesis 3.2. For  t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*)  and  j\ge j_1 , set  \tau_j=c2^{-2j}  and  s=t-\tau_j .

From the Duhamel formula and heat kernel bounds:

\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
e^{-c_0 c}\|\Delta_j u(s)\|_{L^\infty}
+
C_2 2^j
\int_{t-\tau_j}^t
e^{-c_0 2^{2j}(t-r)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^\infty}\,dr.
\tag{4.2}

By Bernstein's inequality,

\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C_3 2^{3j/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^2}.
\tag{4.3}

Hence, multiplying by  2^{-j} ,

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
e^{-c_0 c}N(t)
+
C_2 C_3 2^{3j/2}
\int_{t-\tau_j}^t
e^{-c_0 2^{2j}(t-r)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^2}\,dr.
\tag{4.4}

Applying Hölder's inequality in time and evaluating the exponential integral:

\int_{t-\tau_j}^t
e^{-c_0 2^{2j}(t-r)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^2}\,dr
\le
C_4 2^{-j}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([t-\tau_j,t];L^2)}.
\tag{4.5}

Thus the integral term in (4.4) is bounded by

C_2 C_3 C_4 2^{j/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([t-\tau_j,t];L^2)}.
\tag{4.6}

Now apply Conjecture 3.1:

2^{j/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([t-\tau_j,t];L^2)}
\le
C_5 2^{-j\delta}
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
N(t)
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t).
\tag{4.7}

Substituting into (4.4):

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
e^{-c_0 c}N(t)
+
C_1 2^{-j\delta}
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
N(t)
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t),
\tag{4.8}

where  C_1=C_2C_3C_4C_5 .

Choose  c  so large that  e^{-c_0 c}\le \frac14 . Then, by Hypothesis 3.2,

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
\frac14 N(t)
+
C_1 \cdot \frac{1}{4C_1} N(t)
=
\frac12 N(t)
\tag{4.9}

for all  j\ge j_1 .

For the low frequencies  j<j_1 :

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C_6 2^{j/2}\|u(t)\|_{L^2}
\le
C_6 2^{j_1/2}\|u_0\|_{L^2}
=: C_0.
\tag{4.10}

Then

N(t)
\le
C_0 + \sup_{j\ge j_1} 2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C_0 + \frac12 N(t),
\tag{4.11}

so  N(t)\le 2C_0  for all  t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*) .  \square 

