5. Dyadic Carleson Bilinear Conjecture with Paraproduct Decomposition

5.1. Dyadic Carleson bilinear conjecture

Conjecture 5.1 (Dyadic Carleson frequency-localized bilinear estimate).
There exist absolute constants  C>0 ,  \delta>0 ,  \theta>0 , and  c>0  such that for every divergence-free vector field

u\in L^\infty_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,T);\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy})\cap L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,T);\dot H^1),

for every dyadic cube  Q  of side length  2^{-j}  with  j\ge j_0 , and every  t\in(0,T) , one has

2^{j(\frac12+\delta)}
\left(
\int_{Q^*}
|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C
(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}
M_{\rm dy}(t)\,
\mathcal E_Q(t),
\tag{5.1}

where  Q^*=Q\times(t-\ell(Q)^2,t) ,  \varepsilon_Q(t)=\mathcal E_Q(t)^2 , and  M_{\rm dy}(t)=\sup_{s\le t}\|u(s)\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}} .

5.2. Bony paraproduct decomposition and structural support for Conjecture 5.1

To substantiate Conjecture 5.1, we decompose the bilinear interaction  \Delta_j(u\otimes u)  on a dyadic cube  Q  of side length  \ell(Q)=2^{-j}  using Bony's paraproduct algorithm:

u\otimes u = T_u u + T'_u u + R(u,u),
\tag{5.2}

where

T_u u = \sum_{k\in\mathbb Z} S_{k-2}u \otimes \Delta_k u,
\qquad
T'_u u = \sum_{k\in\mathbb Z} \Delta_k u \otimes S_{k-2}u,

and

R(u,u) = \sum_{k\in\mathbb Z} \Delta_k u \otimes \widetilde{\Delta}_k u,
\qquad
\widetilde{\Delta}_k = \sum_{|k'-k|\le 2}\Delta_{k'}.

Here  S_k u = \sum_{l\le k-3}\Delta_l u .

5.2.1. Control of low-high paraproduct terms

For  \Delta_j(T_u u) , the Fourier support restricts interactions to  k\sim j . The low-frequency factor  S_{j-2}u  is controlled pointwise:

\|S_{j-2}u(s)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
\sum_{l\le j-5}\|\Delta_l u(s)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C M_{\rm dy}(t)\sum_{l\le j-5}2^l
\le
C 2^j M_{\rm dy}(t).
\tag{5.3}

Integrating over  Q^* :

\left(
\int_{Q^*}|\Delta_j(T_u u)(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C\|S_{j-2}u\|_{L^\infty(Q^*)}
\left(
\int_{Q^*}|\Delta_j u(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}

\le
C(2^j M_{\rm dy}(t))(2^{-j}\mathcal E_Q(t))
=
C M_{\rm dy}(t)\mathcal E_Q(t).
\tag{5.4}

Thus the paraproduct terms satisfy Conjecture 5.1 without requiring the dissipation moderator.

5.2.2. Control of the resonant term via sub-dyadic dissipation

The resonant term  \Delta_j R(u,u)  sums over high-frequency interactions  k\ge j-2 . We partition  Q  into  2^{3(k-j)}  sub-dyadic cubes  Q'_\nu\in\mathcal D_k  of side length  2^{-k} :

Q = \bigcup_{\nu=1}^{2^{3(k-j)}} Q'_\nu.
\tag{5.5}

On each sub-cube cylinder  (Q'_\nu)^* , Bernstein and Sobolev embedding give:

\|\Delta_k u\|_{L^4((Q'_\nu)^*)}
\le
C 2^{3k/8}\|\Delta_k u\|_{L^2((Q'_\nu)^*)}
\le
C 2^{-5k/8}\mathcal E_{Q'_\nu}(t).
\tag{5.6}

By Hölder:

\int_{(Q'_\nu)^*}|\Delta_k u\otimes\widetilde{\Delta}_k u|^2\,dy\,ds
\le
C 2^{-5k/2}\varepsilon_{Q'_\nu}(t)^2.
\tag{5.7}

Summing over sub-cubes:

\int_{Q^*}|\Delta_k u\otimes\widetilde{\Delta}_k u|^2\,dy\,ds
\le
C 2^{-5k/2}\sum_{\nu=1}^{2^{3(k-j)}}\varepsilon_{Q'_\nu}(t)^2.
\tag{5.8}

Defining  \mu_k(Q)=\max_\nu\varepsilon_{Q'_\nu}(t) , we have

\sum_{\nu=1}^{2^{3(k-j)}}\varepsilon_{Q'_\nu}(t)^2
\le
\mu_k(Q)\cdot\varepsilon_Q(t).
\tag{5.9}

The dissipation moderator  (1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}  enforces decay on high-frequency concentration, so that

\mu_k(Q)
\le
C(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}M_{\rm dy}(t)^2.
\tag{5.10}

Substituting:

\left(
\int_{Q^*}|\Delta_k u\otimes\widetilde{\Delta}_k u|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C 2^{-5k/4}(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}M_{\rm dy}(t)\mathcal E_Q(t).
\tag{5.11}

Summing over  k\ge j-2 :

\left(
\int_{Q^*}|\Delta_j R(u,u)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C\left(\sum_{k\ge j-2}2^{-5k/4}\right)(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}M_{\rm dy}(t)\mathcal E_Q(t)

=
C 2^{-5j/4}(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}M_{\rm dy}(t)\mathcal E_Q(t).
\tag{5.12}

Multiplying by  2^{j(1/2+\delta)}  with  \delta=3/4 :

2^{j(1/2+3/4)}
\left(
\int_{Q^*}|\Delta_j R(u,u)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}M_{\rm dy}(t)\mathcal E_Q(t).
\tag{5.13}

This supports Conjecture 5.1 with  \delta=3/4 .

5.2.3. Summary

The low-high terms are controlled directly by  M_{\rm dy}(t)\mathcal E_Q(t) . The resonant term is controlled by sub-dyadic dissipation with geometric convergence, yielding a gain of  2^{-5j/4} , which provides  \delta=3/4  in Conjecture 5.1. The dissipation moderator  (1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}  acts specifically on the high-high resonant term.

5.3. Maximal-function non-concentration hypothesis

For  j\ge1 , define the maximal dissipation coefficient

\mathcal M_{\delta}(t,x)
=
\sup_{\substack{Q\in\mathcal D\\ x\in Q\\ \ell(Q)\le 2^{-j_1}}}
2^{-\delta \log_2(1/\ell(Q))}
(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}
\mathcal E_Q(t).
\tag{5.14}

Hypothesis 5.3 (Weak maximal non-concentration).
Let  \delta,\theta  be as in Conjecture 5.1 and let  C_1  be the constant in Lemma 6.1 below. If  T^*<\infty  is a finite maximal time of existence, then there exist  \eta>0 ,  j_1\ge1 , and  \alpha\ge0  such that

\left|
\left\{
(x,t)\in\mathbb R^3\times[T^*-\eta,T^*):
\mathcal M_{\delta}(t,x)
>
\frac{1}{4C_1}
\right\}
\right|
\le
\alpha.
\tag{5.15}

