6. Conditional Continuation Criterion in Dyadic  \mathrm{BMO}^{-1} 

6.1. Dyadic Carleson summation lemma

Lemma 6.1 (Dyadic Carleson summation).
There exists an absolute constant  C>0  such that for every divergence-free tensor  F  with finite local Carleson norm, and for every dyadic cube  Q ,

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C
\sup_{\substack{Q'\in\mathcal D\\ Q'\subset Q}}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|F(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{6.1}

6.2. Off-diagonal heat-kernel decay lemma

The following lemma resolves the nonlocality objection raised in the critical review of Version 5.0.

Lemma 6.2 (Off-diagonal heat-kernel decay).
Let  Q\in\mathcal D  be a dyadic cube of side length  \ell(Q)=2^{-j} . Define the dyadic spatial shells for  k\ge1 :

C_k(Q) = 2^{k+1}Q \setminus 2^k Q.

Let  F(s,x)  be a divergence-free tensor supported in  C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2) . Then for every  (y,t)\in Q^* :

\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F(s,y)\,ds
\right|
\le
C 2^{-k}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)},
\tag{6.2}

where

\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(Q')}
=
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|F(s,x)|^2\,dx\,ds
\right)^{1/2}.

Proof.

Step 1: Pointwise kernel bounds. The kernel  K_\tau(z)  of  e^{\tau\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot  in  \mathbb R^3  satisfies

|K_\tau(z)|
\le
\frac{C}{(\sqrt{\tau}+|z|)^4}
\qquad\text{for all } z\in\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\},\ \tau>0.
\tag{6.3}

This follows from the standard heat kernel bound and the Calderón–Zygmund nature of the Leray projection.

Step 2: Geometry of far-field interactions. Fix  (y,t)\in Q^*  and  x\in C_k(Q) . Then

|y-x|
\ge
\mathrm{dist}(Q,\mathbb R^3\setminus 2^k Q)
\ge
2^{k-1}\ell(Q).

Also  0<\tau=t-s\le\ell(Q)^2 , so  \sqrt{\tau}\le\ell(Q) . Since  k\ge1 , we have  \sqrt{\tau}\le\ell(Q)\ll|y-x|\sim 2^k\ell(Q) . Substituting into (6.3):

|K_{t-s}(y-x)|
\le
\frac{C}{(2^k\ell(Q))^4}
=
\frac{C}{2^{4k}|Q|^{4/3}}.
\tag{6.4}

Step 3: Space-time integration. The Duhamel integral  I_k(t,y)  satisfies:

|I_k(t,y)|
\le
\frac{C}{2^{4k}\ell(Q)^4}
\int_0^{\ell(Q)^2}\int_{C_k(Q)}|F(s,x)|\,dx\,ds.
\tag{6.5}

By Cauchy–Schwarz:

\int_0^{\ell(Q)^2}\int_{C_k(Q)}|F(s,x)|\,dx\,ds
\le
\left(\iint_{C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}1\,dx\,ds\right)^{1/2}
\left(\iint_{C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}|F|^2\,dx\,ds\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{6.6}

Evaluating the volume factor:

\left(|C_k(Q)|\cdot\ell(Q)^2\right)^{1/2}
\le
(C2^{3k}\ell(Q)^3\cdot\ell(Q)^2)^{1/2}
=
C2^{3k/2}\ell(Q)^{5/2}.
\tag{6.7}

For the  L^2  factor, since  C_k(Q)\subset 2^{k+1}Q  and  (0,\ell(Q)^2)\subset(0,\ell(2^{k+1}Q)^2) :

\left(\iint_{C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}|F|^2\,dx\,ds\right)^{1/2}
\le
|2^{k+1}Q|^{1/2}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}
=
C2^{3k/2}\ell(Q)^{3/2}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}.
\tag{6.8}

Step 4: Algebraic summation. Multiplying the two factors:

\int_0^{\ell(Q)^2}\int_{C_k(Q)}|F(s,x)|\,dx\,ds
\le
C2^{3k}\ell(Q)^4\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}.
\tag{6.9}

Substituting into (6.5):

|I_k(t,y)|
\le
\frac{C}{2^{4k}\ell(Q)^4}\cdot C2^{3k}\ell(Q)^4\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}
=
C2^{-k}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}.
\tag{6.10}

 \square 

6.3. Proof of Lemma 6.1 via far-field summation

Decompose any global tensor  F  into a local part  F_0=F\cdot\mathbb{1}_{3Q\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}  and far-field dyadic shells  F_k=F\cdot\mathbb{1}_{C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)} :

F = F_0 + \sum_{k=1}^\infty F_k.
\tag{6.11}

By the local Carleson embedding theorem, the local part satisfies:

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F_0(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C\sup_{Q'\subset 3Q}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(Q')}.
\tag{6.12}

For the far-field summation, applying Lemma 6.2 to each  F_k :

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F_k(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C2^{-k}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}.
\tag{6.13}

Summing over  k\ge1 :

\sum_{k=1}^\infty
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F_k(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C\left(\sum_{k=1}^\infty 2^{-k}\right)\sup_{Q'\in\mathcal D}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(Q')}.
\tag{6.14}

The geometric series  \sum_{k=1}^\infty 2^{-k}=1  guarantees absolute convergence of all far-field spatial interactions. This completes the proof of Lemma 6.1.  \square 

6.4. Conditional dyadic regularity criterion

Proposition 6.3 (Conditional global regularity in dyadic  \mathrm{BMO}^{-1} ).
Assume Conjecture 5.1 holds, Hypothesis 5.3 holds with  \alpha=0 , and Lemmas 6.1–6.2 are valid. Then for every initial datum

u_0\in L^2_\sigma\cap\mathrm{BMO}^{-1},

the Navier–Stokes equations possess a unique global-in-time solution

u\in C([0,\infty);\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy})\cap L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,\infty);\dot H^1).

Moreover,  u\in C^\infty((0,\infty)\times\mathbb R^3) .

Proof outline. The argument follows Version 5.0, with Lemma 6.1 now fully justified by Lemma 6.2. Apply Conjecture 5.1 dyadically, sum over  j\ge j_1  via the factor  2^{-j\delta} , and use Hypothesis 5.3 with  \alpha=0  to close the bootstrap. The low-frequency part is controlled by the energy inequality.  \square 

