Appendix C. Dyadic Carleson Norm and Equivalence with Continuous  \mathrm{BMO}^{-1} 

C.1. Definitions

For a cube  Q\subset\mathbb R^3 , let  \ell(Q)  denote its side length and  Q^*=Q\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)  the associated parabolic cylinder.

The continuous Koch–Tataru norm is

\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}}
=
\sup_{Q\subset\mathbb R^3}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f(y)|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{C.1}

The dyadic variant is

\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}
=
\sup_{Q\in\mathcal D}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f(y)|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{C.2}

C.2. Equivalence

Proposition C.1. There exists an absolute constant  C>0  such that for all  f\in\mathcal S' ,

C^{-1}\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}
\le
\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}}
\le
C\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}.
\tag{C.3}

Proof. The first inequality is trivial because dyadic cubes are a subset of all cubes.

For the second, let  Q  be an arbitrary cube. There exists a dyadic cube  Q_0\in\mathcal D  such that  Q\subset Q_0 ,  \ell(Q_0)\le 2\ell(Q) . Then

Q^*\subset \widetilde Q_0^*

where  \widetilde Q_0  is a cube concentric with  Q_0  and side length  c\ell(Q_0) , with  c\le 4 . The Carleson condition is invariant under fattening of cubes: for any cube  Q  and any constant  A\ge1 ,

\frac{1}{|AQ|}
\int_{(AQ)^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f|^2
\le
C_A
\sup_{Q'\in\mathcal D}
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f|^2.

This follows because  AQ  can be covered by a bounded number of dyadic cubes of comparable size, and the parabolic cylinder  (AQ)^*  by their associated cylinders. Applying with  A=4  and taking the supremum over all cubes  Q  gives the second inequality.  \square 

