Appendix D. Dyadic Carleson Summation Lemma with Off-Diagonal Decay

D.1. Statement of Lemma 6.1

Lemma D.1 (Dyadic Carleson summation).
There exists an absolute constant  C>0  such that for every divergence-free tensor  F  with finite local Carleson norm, and for every dyadic cube  Q ,

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}
\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C
\sup_{\substack{Q'\in\mathcal D\\ Q'\subset Q}}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|F(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{D.1}

D.2. Local Carleson embedding

The standard Carleson embedding theorem gives, for any nonnegative measure  \mu  on  \mathbb R^3\times(0,\infty) ,

\int_{\mathbb R^3\times(0,\infty)}
|e^{t\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F(s)|^2\,d\mu(s,y)
\le
C
\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}}^2
\int_{\mathbb R^3\times(0,\infty)} d\mu,

where  \|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}}  is the global Carleson norm of  F . To localize, take  \mu  to be the restriction of Lebesgue measure to  Q^* . Then

\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F_0(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\le
C
\|F_0\|_{\mathrm{Car}(3Q)}^2
|Q^*|,

where  F_0=F\cdot\mathbb{1}_{3Q\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}  and  \|F_0\|_{\mathrm{Car}(3Q)}  is the local Carleson norm over subcubes of  3Q . Dividing by  |Q|  and taking the square root gives

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F_0(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C
\sup_{Q'\subset 3Q}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|F(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{D.2}

D.3. Off-diagonal heat-kernel decay lemma

Lemma D.2 (Off-diagonal heat-kernel decay).
Let  Q\in\mathcal D  be a dyadic cube of side length  \ell(Q)=2^{-j} . Define the dyadic spatial shells for  k\ge1 :

C_k(Q) = 2^{k+1}Q \setminus 2^k Q.

Let  F(s,x)  be a divergence-free tensor supported in  C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2) . Then for every  (y,t)\in Q^* :

\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F(s,y)\,ds
\right|
\le
C 2^{-k}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)},
\tag{D.3}

where

\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(Q')}
=
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|F(s,x)|^2\,dx\,ds
\right)^{1/2}.

Proof.

Step 1: Pointwise kernel bounds. The kernel  K_\tau(z)  of the composite operator  e^{\tau\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot  in  \mathbb R^3  is given by  K_\tau(z)=\mathbb P\nabla G_\tau(z) , where  G_\tau(z)=(4\pi\tau)^{-3/2}e^{-|z|^2/4\tau}  is the 3D heat kernel and  \mathbb P  is the Leray projection. Because  \mathbb P  is a Calderón–Zygmund singular integral operator with homogeneous kernel  E(z)=\Omega(z/|z|)/|z|^3  ( \int_{\mathbb S^2}\Omega=0 ), a Taylor expansion of  E(z-w)  for  |w|\ll|z|  integrated against  \nabla G_\tau(w)  yields

|K_\tau(z)|
\le
\frac{C}{(\sqrt{\tau}+|z|)^4}
\qquad\text{for all } z\in\mathbb R^3\setminus\{0\},\ \tau>0.
\tag{D.4}

Step 2: Geometry of far-field interactions. Fix  (y,t)\in Q^*  and  x\in C_k(Q) . By construction,

|y-x|
\ge
\mathrm{dist}(Q,\mathbb R^3\setminus 2^k Q)
\ge
2^{k-1}\ell(Q).

Also  0<\tau=t-s\le\ell(Q)^2 , so  \sqrt{\tau}\le\ell(Q) . Since  k\ge1 ,  \sqrt{\tau}\le\ell(Q)\ll|y-x|\sim 2^k\ell(Q) . Substituting into (D.4):

|K_{t-s}(y-x)|
\le
\frac{C}{(2^k\ell(Q))^4}
=
\frac{C}{2^{4k}|Q|^{4/3}}.
\tag{D.5}

Step 3: Space-time integration. Using (D.5), the Duhamel integral  I_k(t,y)=\int_0^t\int_{C_k(Q)}K_{t-s}(y-x)F(s,x)\,dx\,ds  satisfies:

|I_k(t,y)|
\le
\frac{C}{2^{4k}\ell(Q)^4}
\int_0^{\ell(Q)^2}\int_{C_k(Q)}|F(s,x)|\,dx\,ds.
\tag{D.6}

Applying Cauchy–Schwarz to the space-time integral over  C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2) :

\int_0^{\ell(Q)^2}\int_{C_k(Q)}|F(s,x)|\,dx\,ds
\le
\left(\iint_{C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}1\,dx\,ds\right)^{1/2}
\left(\iint_{C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}|F|^2\,dx\,ds\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{D.7}

Evaluating each factor:

Volume factor:

\left(|C_k(Q)|\cdot\ell(Q)^2\right)^{1/2}
\le
\left(C2^{3k}\ell(Q)^3\cdot\ell(Q)^2\right)^{1/2}
=
C2^{3k/2}\ell(Q)^{5/2}.
\tag{D.8}

 L^2  energy factor: Since  C_k(Q)\subset 2^{k+1}Q  and  (0,\ell(Q)^2)\subset(0,\ell(2^{k+1}Q)^2) ,

\left(\iint_{C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}|F|^2\,dx\,ds\right)^{1/2}
\le
\left(\int_{(2^{k+1}Q)^*}|F|^2\,dx\,ds\right)^{1/2}
=
|2^{k+1}Q|^{1/2}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}

=
C2^{3k/2}\ell(Q)^{3/2}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}.
\tag{D.9}

Step 4: Algebraic summation. Multiplying (D.8) and (D.9):

\int_0^{\ell(Q)^2}\int_{C_k(Q)}|F(s,x)|\,dx\,ds
\le
C2^{3k}\ell(Q)^4\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}.
\tag{D.10}

Substituting (D.10) into (D.6):

|I_k(t,y)|
\le
\frac{C}{2^{4k}\ell(Q)^4}\cdot C2^{3k}\ell(Q)^4\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}
=
C2^{-k}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}.
\tag{D.11}

This completes the proof of Lemma D.2.  \square 

D.4. Proof of Lemma D.1 via far-field summation

Decompose any global tensor  F  into a local part  F_0=F\cdot\mathbb{1}_{3Q\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}  and far-field dyadic shells  F_k=F\cdot\mathbb{1}_{C_k(Q)\times(0,\ell(Q)^2)}  for  k\ge1 :

F = F_0 + \sum_{k=1}^\infty F_k.
\tag{D.12}

By the local Carleson embedding (D.2), the local part satisfies:

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F_0(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C\sup_{Q'\subset 3Q}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(Q')}.
\tag{D.13}

For the far-field summation, applying Lemma D.2 to each  F_k :

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F_k(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C2^{-k}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(2^{k+1}Q)}.
\tag{D.14}

Summing over all dyadic shells  k\ge1 :

\sum_{k=1}^\infty
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F_k(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C\left(\sum_{k=1}^\infty 2^{-k}\right)\sup_{Q'\in\mathcal D}\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(Q')}.
\tag{D.15}

The geometric series  \sum_{k=1}^\infty 2^{-k}=1  guarantees absolute convergence of all far-field spatial interactions. Combining (D.13) and (D.15) yields (D.1). This completes the proof of Lemma D.1.  \square 

