Appendix G. Axisymmetric Program: Local  \Gamma  Bound and Energy Inequality

G.1. Equation for  \Gamma 

In the no-swirl axisymmetric case, the velocity field is  u=u_r e_r+u_z e_z  and the vorticity is  \omega=\omega^\phi e_\phi , where  \omega^\phi=\partial_z u_r-\partial_r u_z . The scalar  \Gamma=\omega^\phi/r  satisfies

\partial_t\Gamma + u\cdot\nabla\Gamma - \Delta_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma - \frac{2}{r}\partial_r\Gamma = 0.
\tag{G.1}

Observing that

\Delta_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma + \frac{2}{r}\partial_r\Gamma
=
\partial_r^2\Gamma + \frac{3}{r}\partial_r\Gamma + \partial_z^2\Gamma
=
\Delta_{\mathbb R^5}\Gamma,

equation (G.1) represents a 5-dimensional convective diffusion process. The global maximum principle guarantees

\|\Gamma(t)\|_{L^\infty(\mathbb R^3)}
\le
\|\Gamma_0\|_{L^\infty(\mathbb R^3)}
\qquad\text{for all } t\ge0.
\tag{G.2}

G.2. Local Carleson bound on dyadic cylinders

Let  Q\in\mathcal D  be a dyadic cube of side length  \ell(Q)=2R=2^{-j}  centered on the symmetry axis  r=0  at  z=z_0 . The parabolic cylinder is  Q^*=Q\times(t_0-4R^2,t_0) .

Theorem G.1 (Unconditional local  \Gamma  bound).
There exists an absolute constant  C>0  such that

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
|\Gamma(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}
\le
\ell(Q)\|\Gamma_0\|_{L^\infty}
=
2^{-j}\|\Gamma_0\|_{L^\infty}.
\tag{G.3}

Proof. By direct space-time integration:

\int_{Q^*}|\Gamma(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\le
\|\Gamma_0\|_{L^\infty}^2\int_{t_0-4R^2}^{t_0}\int_Q 1\,dy\,ds
=
\|\Gamma_0\|_{L^\infty}^2\cdot|Q|\cdot(4R^2).

Dividing by  |Q|  and taking the square root:

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}|\Gamma(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}
\le
2R\|\Gamma_0\|_{L^\infty}
=
\ell(Q)\|\Gamma_0\|_{L^\infty}.

 \square 

G.3. Local energy inequality and dissipation bound

Let  \eta(r,z,t)=\phi(r)\psi(z)\chi(t)  be a smooth test function such that:

·  \phi(r)=1  for  r\le R ,  \phi(r)=0  for  r\ge 2R , with  |\partial_r\phi|\le C/R  and  \partial_r\phi\equiv0  near  r=0 .
·  \psi(z)=1  for  |z-z_0|\le R ,  \psi(z)=0  for  |z-z_0|\ge 2R , with  |\partial_z\psi|\le C/R .
·  \chi(t)=0  for  t\le t_0-4R^2 ,  \chi(t)=1  for  t\ge t_0-R^2 , with  0\le\partial_t\chi\le C/R^2 .

Set  \Phi=\eta^2 . Note that  \frac{1}{r}\partial_r\Phi=\frac{2\eta}{r}\partial_r\eta  is smooth and bounded by  C/R^2  because  \partial_r\eta=0  near  r=0 .

Multiply (G.1) by  \Gamma\Phi  and integrate over  \mathbb R^3 :

\int_{\mathbb R^3}(\partial_t\Gamma)\Gamma\Phi\,dx
+
\int_{\mathbb R^3}(u\cdot\nabla\Gamma)\Gamma\Phi\,dx
-
\int_{\mathbb R^3}\left(\Delta_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma+\frac{2}{r}\partial_r\Gamma\right)\Gamma\Phi\,dx
=
0.
\tag{G.4}

We integrate each term separately.

Time derivative:

\int_{\mathbb R^3}(\partial_t\Gamma)\Gamma\Phi\,dx
=
\frac12\frac{d}{dt}\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma^2\Phi\,dx
-
\frac12\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma^2\partial_t\Phi\,dx.
\tag{G.5}

Incompressible convection ( \nabla\cdot u=0 ):

\int_{\mathbb R^3}(u\cdot\nabla\Gamma)\Gamma\Phi\,dx
=
-\frac12\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma^2(u\cdot\nabla\Phi)\,dx.
\tag{G.6}

Diffusion and radial drift: Using  dx=2\pi r\,dr\,dz , integration by parts yields

\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Delta_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma\cdot\Gamma\Phi\,dx
=
-\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\nabla\Gamma|^2\Phi\,dx
-
\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma\nabla\Gamma\cdot\nabla\Phi\,dx,
\tag{G.7}

and

\int_{\mathbb R^3}\frac{2}{r}\partial_r\Gamma\cdot\Gamma\Phi\,dx
=
2\pi\int_{-\infty}^\infty\int_0^\infty 2\partial_r\left(\frac{\Gamma^2}{2}\right)\Phi\,dr\,dz
=
-\int_{\mathbb R^3}\frac{\Gamma^2}{r}\partial_r\Phi\,dx.
\tag{G.8}

Combining (G.5)–(G.8) into (G.4):

\frac12\frac{d}{dt}\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma^2\Phi\,dx
+
\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\nabla\Gamma|^2\Phi\,dx
=
\frac12\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma^2\left(\partial_t\Phi+u\cdot\nabla\Phi-\frac{2}{r}\partial_r\Phi\right)dx
-
\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma\nabla\Gamma\cdot\nabla\Phi\,dx.
\tag{G.9}

By Cauchy–Schwarz with  \epsilon=1/2 :

\left|\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma\nabla\Gamma\cdot\nabla\Phi\,dx\right|
\le
\frac12\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\nabla\Gamma|^2\Phi\,dx
+
\frac12\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma^2\frac{|\nabla\Phi|^2}{\Phi}\,dx.
\tag{G.10}

Substituting (G.10) into (G.9) and using  \frac{|\nabla\Phi|^2}{\Phi}=4|\nabla\eta|^2 :

\frac{d}{dt}\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma^2\eta^2\,dx
+
\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\nabla\Gamma|^2\eta^2\,dx
\le
\int_{\mathbb R^3}\Gamma^2\left(|\partial_t\Phi|+4|\nabla\eta|^2+\frac{2}{r}|\partial_r\Phi|+|u\cdot\nabla\Phi|\right)dx.
\tag{G.11}

Integrating in time over  (t_0-4R^2,t_0) , using the bounds on  \eta  and the global  L^\infty  bound (G.2):

\int_{t_0-R^2}^{t_0}\int_Q|\nabla\Gamma(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\le
C\left(1+R\|u\|_{L^\infty(Q^*)}\right)\|\Gamma_0\|_{L^\infty}^2|Q|.
\tag{G.12}

This provides the first unconditional verification of the dyadic Carleson framework in the no-swirl axisymmetric setting.

