Appendix H. Wavelet Carleson Characterization

H.1. Divergence-free wavelet basis

A divergence-free orthonormal wavelet basis of  L^2_\sigma(\mathbb R^3)  consists of functions  \{\psi_\lambda\}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}  with wavelet-type localization, such that  \nabla\cdot\psi_\lambda=0  in the sense of distributions, and the family is an orthonormal basis of the solenoidal subspace.

H.2. Dyadic Carleson characterization

Hypothesis H.1. There exists such a basis and a constant  C>0  such that for all  f\in\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy} ,

C^{-1}\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}
\le
\sup_{Q\in\mathcal D}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\sum_{\lambda:\,\mathrm{supp}\,\psi_\lambda\subset Q}
|c_\lambda|^2
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}.
\tag{H.1}

H.3. Necessary conditions

For (H.1) to hold, the wavelet basis must satisfy:

1. Unconditional basis property for  \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy} , stronger than the usual Besov characterization.
2. Carleson measure condition: wavelet coefficients must be summable so that for each dyadic cube  Q , the sum of  |c_\lambda|^2  over wavelets supported in  Q  is comparable to the Carleson average of the heat extension.
3. Divergence-free constraint: the projection onto the solenoidal subspace must commute with the wavelet decomposition, at least approximately.

H.4. Open problems

Even if Hypothesis H.1 is true, the trilinear wavelet estimate needed to prove Conjecture 5.1 remains open. The main difficulty is the interaction of three wavelet scales: the bilinear term  u\otimes u  produces wavelets at scales comparable to the input scales, but the dissipation moderator involves a local average that is not diagonal in the wavelet basis.

