Appendix I. Critical Analysis and Open Ingredients

I.1. Logical structure

Theorem A (proved): If Conjecture 3.1 and Hypothesis 3.2 hold, then the Besov norm  \dot{B}^{-1}_{\infty,\infty}  is bounded near any putative singularity. Proof is complete in Appendix B.

Proposition 6.3 (conditional regularity): If Conjecture 5.1, Hypothesis 5.3 with  \alpha=0 , and Lemmas 6.1–6.2 hold, then global regularity follows. Proof outline is complete; relies on Lemma 6.1.

I.2. Status of ingredients

1. Conjecture 5.1 — open. The paraproduct decomposition in Appendix F provides structural support with  \delta=3/4 , but the control of  \mu_k(Q)  via the dissipation moderator remains an assumption.
2. Lemma 6.1 — now fully proved modulo the local Carleson embedding, with off-diagonal decay handled by Lemma 6.2.
3. Hypothesis 5.3 — open, falsifiable, measure-theoretic.
4. Hypothesis H.1 — open; wavelet Carleson characterization for  \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy} .

I.3. Strengths of Version 5.1

· Nonlocality objection resolved by explicit off-diagonal kernel decay.
· Paraproduct decomposition gives a concrete mechanism for the bilinear conjecture with  \delta=3/4 .
· Axisymmetric benchmark provides an unconditional dyadic Carleson bound.

I.4. Remaining vulnerabilities

· The local Carleson embedding in Appendix D.2 is assumed; a complete proof requires the T(1) theorem for Carleson measures on dyadic cylinders.
· The control of  \mu_k(Q)  in Appendix F.3 via the dissipation moderator is not derived from the equations; it remains part of Conjecture 5.1.
· For  \alpha>0 , the passage from the maximal non-concentration condition to global regularity requires coupling with CKN theory, which is not addressed.

I.5. Possible weakenings

1. Replace  M_{\rm dy}(t)  by  N(t)  in Conjecture 5.1.
2. Allow logarithmic loss in Lemma 6.1.
3. Use logarithmic dissipation moderator  (\log(e+\varepsilon_Q^{-1}))^{-\theta} .
4. Allow  \delta<3/4 .

No unconditional regularity is claimed. The revised framework is logically coherent, technically precise, and identifies concrete, falsifiable targets for future research.